WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.815
Unsafe Rust is a disciplined engineering
tool.

2
00:00:02.895 --> 00:00:06.773
Use it only when safe Rust cannot express
the required behavior clearly enough.

3
00:00:06.853 --> 00:00:10.036
Before accepting unsafe code, ask what
low-level need it serves,

4
00:00:10.116 --> 00:00:13.936
why a safe alternative is not sufficient,
and whether the scope can be smaller.

5
00:00:14.016 --> 00:00:16.100
Unsafe should earn its place in the
design.

6
00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:19.247
Keep unsafe code small, isolated,
justified, and documented.

7
00:00:19.327 --> 00:00:22.337
Treat every unsafe block as a boundary
with a safety contract.

8
00:00:22.417 --> 00:00:26.295
The contract should explain preconditions,
invariants, caller obligations,

9
00:00:26.375 --> 00:00:29.848
ownership expectations, lifetime
assumptions, thread assumptions,

10
00:00:29.928 --> 00:00:31.953
layout assumptions, and error behavior.

11
00:00:32.033 --> 00:00:34.754
A comment that merely says the code is
safe is not enough.

12
00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:38.248
Review raw pointers, aliasing, lifetimes,
layout, mutable statics,

13
00:00:38.328 --> 00:00:40.643
unsafe traits, and FFI assumptions
carefully.

14
00:00:40.723 --> 00:00:44.891
Prefer safe wrappers that protect
invariants and prevent ordinary safe callers from

15
00:00:44.971 --> 00:00:46.475
breaking unsafe assumptions.

16
00:00:46.555 --> 00:00:50.549
The best unsafe abstraction makes the
difficult reasoning local while exposing a

17
00:00:50.629 --> 00:00:52.828
clear, safe interface to the rest of the
code.

18
00:00:52.908 --> 00:00:54.934
Finally, maintain unsafe code over time.

19
00:00:55.014 --> 00:00:56.924
Test boundaries and regression cases.

20
00:00:57.004 --> 00:00:58.625
Use review tooling where it fits.

21
00:00:58.705 --> 00:01:01.714
Update safety documentation when
surrounding code changes.

22
00:01:01.794 --> 00:01:05.672
Revisit unsafe areas as dependencies,
platforms, compilers, and APIs evolve.

23
00:01:05.752 --> 00:01:07.836
The goal is to use, review, document,
test,

24
00:01:07.916 --> 00:01:11.620
and maintain unsafe Rust as a small,
justified, controlled trust boundary.
